# Every layer some operation induces into, which is the `categoryValue` population. One row per
# (filing, layer) that N reaches, and nothing else.
#
# N gets a notation
#   N was once filed as totally lost. `induction` mapped to `laneSet`, the same element as `draw`,
#   and was deliberately not emitted: a second lane set gives every layer two `lane` elements with
#   nothing but a matching name to say they are one layer, so the conformed key `(filing, layer)`
#   does not survive. That reasoning was right about lane sets and wrong about BPMN, which has a
#   second mechanism entirely.
#
# A category is not a partition
#   That is exactly why it fits. `tFlowElement` carries `categoryValueRef` with
#   `maxOccurs="unbounded"`, so a flow element may be in many categories, where `flowNodeRef` puts
#   it in one lane. D is a containment and N is a cover, and BPMN has one element for each.
#   Emitting N as a second lane set would have added lanes and broken `|lane| = |layer|`; this
#   adds none.
#
# What it loses
#   This file is not the end of the argument. `tCategoryValue` carries a `value` of type
#   `xs:string` and no reference attribute of any kind, so the layer arrives as text beside a
#   `lane` of the same name and nothing in the document ties the two. It is the weaker cousin of
#   the `callActivity` defect: a call loses the layer outright, a category keeps its name and loses
#   the pointer.
#
# One row per layer, not per induction
#   A categoryValue is one element however many operations reference it.
#   diagrams/category_members.sqlc is the per-edge population.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- pm:Operation/pm:Induction projected to the layers it reaches; BPMN 2.0 tCategoryValue.
WITH
inductions AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/inductions.sqlc)
)
SELECT DISTINCT i.filing, i.layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) i
